    "note": "original Q95 kept, not retracted -- diagnosed as prior-driven (U(0,0.21) on 190 real GPi2 gates leaves only 1.7% of prior support with >50% signal survival, an analytic fact independent of sample size). HOWEVER the EMPIRICAL quantile comparison does NOT cleanly confirm GPi2-alone as the tail's cause: pinning p_GPi2 to GPi's tight measured range gave Q95=61.07, Q99=701.82 -- SAME ORDER OF MAGNITUDE as the original (51.22/888.29), not the dramatic collapse the prior-survival argument alone would suggest. Read honestly: (a) both runs are heavy-tailed distributions estimated from only ~100-120 draws, where Q95/Q99 are themselves high-variance statistics -- this comparison is UNDERPOWERED to distinguish 'GPi2 was most of the story' from 'something else (p_zz_true, angle bias, calibration ratio) drives the tail regardless of GPi2', (b) Task 32E's larger, per-parameter-vector design (saving every draw's full parameter vector + Spearman/partial-rank/Sobol sensitivity) is the experiment that actually answers this, not this quick rerun. This correction's real, sample-size-independent contribution is the analytic prior-survival finding; the empirical requantification is suggestive but inconclusive pending Task 32E."
